#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184930} :activity/id #uuid "c358bde0-c269-53bb-bce4-8d51f4516e17" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185317 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we've seen how some sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication operations exhibit properties similar to $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nIn this lesson, we will formally define what we mean by a **vector space.**\n\nFirst, let $V$ be a set equipped with the following two operations:\n\nVector addition: \n\nFor any two elements $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V,$ there is a unique element $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under vector addition.**\n\nScalar multiplication: \n\nFor any element $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ and any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R},$ there is a unique element $\\alpha \\mathbf{x}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under scalar multiplication.**\n\nA set $V$ equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication forms a **vector space** if it satisfies the following **vector space axioms**. \n\nAxioms of Vector Addition ($+$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A1]}$: $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} =  \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$; vector addition is **commutative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A2]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z} =  \\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}, \\mathbf{z} \\in V$; vectors addition is **associative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A3]}$: There exists a **zero-vector** $\\mathbf{0}$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{x}$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A4]}$: For every $\\mathbf{x} \\in V,$ there exists $-\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + (-\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}$; $-\\mathbf{x}$ is called the **negative** of $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nAxioms of Scalar Multiplication ($\\cdot$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A5]}$: $\\color{blue}1 \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$; multiplying by $1$ does not change a vector.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A6]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\nAxioms Connecting Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication (the Distributive Laws)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A7]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{x} = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\beta \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A8]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{y}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\alpha \\mathbf{y}$ for any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$.\n\nSo, a vector space is simply a collective term for all kinds of sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication, where these operations satisfy this particular list of axioms (which are similar to the properties of these operations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$). \n\nThe elements of any vector space are called **vectors**." :tutorial/id #uuid "488db0ee-92f8-5790-8ff6-2977ab0e6dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3187 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "708bbadc-ca98-5148-98dd-f1cc4b9125d0" :step/math-academy-id 653101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184065 :question/id #uuid "2cb88713-f830-501e-9b0a-60320936ad14" :question/math-academy-id "e-4792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442957}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"} :step/id #uuid "0564ce26-4a8b-562e-9ab6-4940e223854b" :step/math-academy-id 653106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184196 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184094 :question/id #uuid "132efc65-5f50-5582-8955-a5d34c57af04" :question/math-academy-id "e-8224"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442959}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "ca7e22db-059b-547f-ad06-b3fe2c740ff4" :step/math-academy-id 653103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185345 :tutorial/content "There are many different vector spaces. Some of the most important are listed below.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^n$, the set of all column vectors of height $n$ with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_n(\\mathbb{R})$, the set of all $n \\times n$ matrices with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the set of all polynomials in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$, the set of all polynomials of degree less than or equal to $n$ in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The set of all real-valued functions of one variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "eb7bd53e-69ec-5e14-b970-1bd6ff9aff7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6199 :tutorial/title "Important Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "2b2df015-d32e-5a46-ab00-33e8ccdd4686" :step/math-academy-id 653102 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184969}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184420}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419008} :activity/id #uuid "71036dcd-e882-527a-9681-dd5cc25371dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54174a8e-4b9f-5398-9c3e-042309cf5af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} {:answer/id #uuid "7c55b42e-8e25-5d4a-9c6c-fb23853f6637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419001} {:answer/id #uuid "923e4cd4-7cb7-513f-a013-6b459182a21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419002} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a112ad-76a7-5358-b6fe-7be7dc9072ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419003} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa960c-9666-5ef7-ae8f-8608f1b827e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418998} :answer-field/id #uuid "90535267-04a3-5209-88fc-9a5564318779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19147c4b-722c-5ed5-9d91-8a4847b759ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-35626" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=0.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f729f32-9ccf-5071-9aab-348325a597e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313cdc82-5d69-5af4-95bb-ace914a012ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f43eb35-6698-556b-937a-7540b2eaa194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418997} {:answer/id #uuid "54d7d70f-b06b-5dba-8920-ce4be2335648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418999} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff392f-4de3-5c34-9123-789573d772b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419000} {:answer/id #uuid "f187a6fa-78fb-565f-9a52-c1915abb65f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions  in $A$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4628fceb-08d9-5ba5-86ad-00d444e18706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a47334c-0a05-5b9c-b7eb-76cf4c1d5cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94792" :question/problem "Let $A$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form  $f(x)=a+{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because **multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)={x}^{2},$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin A$ because the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in A.$ $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "4418ef90-81d3-58b0-a3d7-e0c226069037"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1337dd4f-9280-53a2-a0bf-10fa5a1172ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385326} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b06213-6b70-5fb1-9074-bd29c49e8d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A4}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385327} {:answer/id #uuid "175edfb7-a947-56df-a366-83319bf965d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385328} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ae0dc-f023-5eac-8d6f-6539fcd8b68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385329} {:answer/id #uuid "f383b979-fa8e-5c2b-8977-e24abfd545ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385328} :answer-field/id #uuid "6de75fa4-40ed-54ed-9a38-aaf2ba31ee58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7e8b0d2-1b58-5e69-8702-c53196883e47" :question/math-academy-id "q-35627" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $w$ is a vector such that $x+w=0$, then $w=-x$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8de51b15-aacb-5258-b556-fea43597fd8c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419009}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418995}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423673} :activity/id #uuid "ea1bef1c-e09c-5c2e-9d55-d2d0c3cb38cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064 :topic/id #uuid "d6e3ec39-fd1a-5be7-9200-cb1c6c27e338" :topic/math-academy-id 3835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96cab4fe-b011-5072-be3e-391125bf75c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399589} {:answer/id #uuid "479eb2f9-8717-57b1-9f07-9c964bbfdbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399590} {:answer/id #uuid "fcde83fb-02ab-5892-a45a-6c4b43adb9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A7}$, $\\text{A5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399591} {:answer/id #uuid "e44c72a4-6813-558a-a34c-560c1aff92da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399592} {:answer/id #uuid "afcd5ca7-6625-5f48-ac5f-5ce7f390cf1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A6}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399592} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb9d1c7-c909-596f-a04a-7d5c7e74340d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "258def85-3c26-5841-81d2-c1a1f3c916a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106944" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*$0\\cdot 0=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =(0+1)\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A7}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{addition of integers}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A5}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A5}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =(0+1)\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A7}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =1\\cdot 0 &  & [\\,\\text{addition of integers}\\,] \\\\ 0\\cdot 0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A5}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0e561cf1-bbce-5b45-99d8-35b7ea403736"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c610fab-a6ec-5612-9143-78410e0c0685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of matrices in $T$ is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423660} {:answer/id #uuid "952805ec-db79-5003-ab44-c608da27648b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "there exists a matrix $A\\in T$ such that $A+B\\ne O$ for any $B\\in T.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423662} {:answer/id #uuid "97a1f3dc-dc3c-53f9-b457-30bca1c9f80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$T$ does not contain the zero matrix $O$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423663} {:answer/id #uuid "d008fb5f-392d-5a1e-8353-f48e8e0933c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of matrices in $T$ is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423665} {:answer/id #uuid "f89006ab-36a7-5769-850c-8ee5d58a71d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423667} :answer-field/id #uuid "047ee837-df71-5e5c-bcee-335a715a816d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87c89c45-3edb-5cd5-88f1-a3ee2b4d8003" :question/math-academy-id "q-35640" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices $({a}_{ij})$  with real entries, such that  ${a}_{11}+{a}_{22}$ is rational.  Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n*$T$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of matrices and multiplication of matrices by a real number) because **multiplication by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\n For example, the identity matrix \n\n$$\nI=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nlies in $T$, but\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cdot I=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\notin T\n$$\n\nbecause $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2}$ is not a rational number.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- The zero matrix belongs to $T.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is commutative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices with real entries is associative, in general.    $\\,✓$\n\n- Given any $A\\in T,$  the additive inverse matrix $-A=(-1)\\cdot A$ is also in  $T,$ and   $A+(-A)=O.$  $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "467837ce-9aea-5bd2-99a0-3354bf395104" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2283d3-68c5-5fca-946c-408c6fb2b791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423658} {:answer/id #uuid "506393e5-9110-55a4-ad28-86b41af5f7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423659} {:answer/id #uuid "94a3dcc7-829d-5b5b-838b-243af61af1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423661} {:answer/id #uuid "b61ef8ae-4ec6-5626-8687-752c08d8c0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423664} {:answer/id #uuid "f44ae4d3-7509-5333-9443-9a689afb980d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{A1}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423659} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e9d841-9401-5444-9a3a-2b082af4b7ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d091317-3092-5633-8a6e-4652d2fd3389" :question/math-academy-id "q-106941" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $u$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+u=x+v$ for some $x\\in V,$ then $u=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+u & =x+v &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+u)+(-x) & =(x+v)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (u+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ u+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,] \\\\ u+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ u & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bbaac633-758e-54a8-bb41-3769e90b4fe8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313cdc82-5d69-5af4-95bb-ace914a012ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f43eb35-6698-556b-937a-7540b2eaa194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418997} {:answer/id #uuid "54d7d70f-b06b-5dba-8920-ce4be2335648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $A$  is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418999} {:answer/id #uuid "61ff392f-4de3-5c34-9123-789573d772b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419000} {:answer/id #uuid "f187a6fa-78fb-565f-9a52-c1915abb65f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions  in $A$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4628fceb-08d9-5ba5-86ad-00d444e18706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a47334c-0a05-5b9c-b7eb-76cf4c1d5cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94792" :question/problem "Let $A$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form  $f(x)=a+{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$A$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication) because **multiplication of $f\\in A$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)={x}^{2},$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin A$ because the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in A.$ $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "f72bd130-061d-5d95-9582-1d1554fa5cad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0fcd6c1-3e34-5869-bda3-f3935c249cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in L$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396601} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae69b77-733b-5c58-bc2b-3800bcfe26dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396602} {:answer/id #uuid "dac8d022-edf8-56b5-99c7-7f2b00e051d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $L$ is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396603} {:answer/id #uuid "6358779e-1da8-5238-9ef6-81ac25fc065f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions in $L$ is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396604} {:answer/id #uuid "85442eb9-7db8-592f-9879-ddfd77d97231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09dfa2c-f035-539f-94db-4698ecc43f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15beea94-47dc-575a-be81-e2d3b2547e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106930" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "f97b0645-0a69-5367-a434-1456880e7389" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423674}}] :activity/title "Defining Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423657}]}
